  "image_name": "1b447fa29ed7792330251093dfcf0f21.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure compares four tectonic reconstructions proposed to explain how the Himalayan region formed within a “Greater India” setting. Each panel shows the same broad geography—Africa, India, Eurasia, Australia, and Antarctica—so the key message is the different ways oceanic crust and continental fragments between India and Eurasia might have been consumed before final collision.\n\nAcross the models, a curved belt labeled the Gangdese Arc marks the southern Eurasian margin, while colored lines indicate plate boundaries, subduction zones, and spreading centers in the surrounding Indian Ocean. The main contrast is what lay north of India before collision. In the “Greater India” and “single-stage collision” ideas, India occupies a larger northern extent, implying direct convergence with Eurasia after ocean closure. In the “intra-oceanic subduction” and “back-arc basin” models, an additional oceanic or arc system appears between India and Eurasia, suggesting a more complex, multi-step closure involving an intra-oceanic arc or back-arc basin, often labeled Neo-Tethys.\n\nOverall, the figure illustrates alternative mechanisms for Himalayan assembly: simple continent–continent collision versus collision preceded by one or more oceanic subduction systems.",
